etcd Backup and Restore

What a snapshot is

etcdctl snapshot save asks a running etcd member to write a point-in-time copy of its bbolt database (the member/snap/db file from your notes) to a single file. It is consistent, so you can take it while the cluster is live.

What is and isn’t in a backup

Backed up
All Kubernetes objects (Deployments, Secrets, ConfigMaps, RBAC, etc.)
Cluster state

Not backed up
Contents of PersistentVolumes (your app data)
Certificates in /etc/kubernetes/pki (back these up separately)
Static pod manifests in /etc/kubernetes/manifests

## So restoring etcd brings back the definitions of things, not the data inside your volumes.

What restore actually does

Restore does not touch the running etcd. 
It takes the snapshot file and builds a brand-new data directory from it. 
Then you point etcd at that new directory. 
Restore also creates a new cluster identity (new cluster ID and member ID), 
so the restored data can’t accidentally rejoin an old cluster.

snapshot save  ->  (disaster)  ->  snapshot restore --data-dir /var/lib/etcd-restore
   ->  edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml (hostPath -> new dir)
   ->  kubelet notices the manifest change and recreates the etcd static pod
   ->  API server reconnects
# Anything created after the snapshot is lost after restore. You are going back in time.

